A||||3||Brian84SE||4 cyl. engine swap||||1||
Z||000000||Brian84SE||05-13-2000||05:42 AM||||I am eventually going to replace the duke in my 84. I read somewhere that I can use a 84-86.(87 went to DIS) Can I use any  2.5 from some other GM car? A friend also offered  me a 2.5 from a 1980 jeep.  What would I have to swap over to use that one? I assume head at the very least.||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||05-13-2000||11:58 AM||||The jeep motor may not work. AMC often modified engines in ways that make them diferant enough to be a problem. (Like Crysler did with their version of the VW rabbit which still had a Audi motor but shoved a chrysler distributor in it.)<P>All the GM FWD 2.5's from that time period will work. You will need to change some brackets and pulleys.||||reg||1
Z||000002||WKDFIRO||05-14-2000||03:13 PM||||Brackets and pulleys and cables and harnesses. The 85 - 88 Fieros had a terminal that was mounted right next to battery. I don't beleive in 84 it was used. It is the c-500 connector. It might be the onlything that slows you down from using the DIS motor. That and the cables are different based on the different linkage of the throttle. Have fun replacing the accelerator cable....YUCK!||||reg||1
Z||000003||DRH||05-15-2000||02:49 PM||||I successfully transplanted a 2.5 from an 83 Phoenix to an 84 Fiero. Just had to use some things like the throttle cable bracket and all the wiring from the Fiero.<P>If you go back far enough the 2.5 was not a crossflow head (intake and exhaust were on the same side). Not sure what year this happened but the pre-crossflow would definitely not fit. (Not sure if you can swap heads but I would doubt it) Also, check that all neccessary bolt holes for mounting and accessories are there. An original RWD motor (like the jeep) will probably also have the starter on the wrong side as well as some other mounting bolt differences.<P>Bottom line. Check it out <B>carefully</B> before jumping in with both feet...<P>Added: ogre is right, any 83-86 FWD motor should work using the right Fiero brackets. 82 or older FWD... maybe, not sure how far back though. Any RWD will probably not work.<P><p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 05-15-2000).]||||reg||1
